# cluster.logi: an A2A agent that controls a cluster of computers for language-model jobs.
#   logi try cluster.logi --observe     logi serve cluster.logi --observe --translator claude-cli --device ./launcher
# The agent LOOKS at the cluster itself (observe_cluster.py); the client (an LLM, or another agent) only asks:
# where can a job run, which node has the most ram or disk, and finally "get j1 running on n3".
name: cluster
about: cluster of computers for language-model jobs

== 1. conversation
client: what can I ask?
agent:  You can ask me, for example
agent:  where can j1 run?
client: where can j1 run?
agent:  j1 is runnable on n3
client: which node has the most ram?
agent:  n3 has the most ram
client: which node has the most disk?
agent:  n3 has the most disk
client: where can j3 run?
agent:  To answer that I need to know: How many GB of ram does j3 need to run?
client: j3 needs 4 GB of ram
agent:  This is what I understood:
agent:  j3 needs 4 GB of ram
client: yes
agent:  j3 is runnable on n3
client: why is j1 too big for n2?
agent:  n2 has only 64 GB of ram and j1 needs 120
client: get j1 running on n2
agent:  I cannot make it true by myself
client: get j1 running on n3
agent:  Done: j1 is running on n3 (command: launch j1 n3)
client: put j1 on the best node
agent:  I did not understand that
agent:  You can ask me, for example

== 2. facts
# What the agent sees, and the structure every resource shares: a node HAS an amount of a resource, a job NEEDS it
# (ram, disk; later gpu: add it to R). The arguments are TYPED (N:name, R:ram|disk, G:int>=0): a fact that does not fit is
# refused before it reaches the rules. Nobody types these: the observe command prints them.
node(N)           = {N} is a node
job(J)            = {J} is a job
capacity(N:name, R:ram|disk, G:int>=0) = {N} has {G} GB of {R}
demand(J:name, R:ram|disk, G:int>=0)   = {J} needs {G} GB of {R}
runs(J, N)        = {J} is running on {N}      [changes]
observe: python3 observe_cluster.py

== 3. rules
# What follows from the facts. The words of each answer come first.
runnable(J, N) = {J} is runnable on {N} | where can {J} run{N=?}
too_big(J, N)  = {J} is too big for {N}
short(J, N, R) = {N} lacks the {R} of {J}
most(N, R)     = {N} has the most {R}
runnable(J, N) :- job(J), node(N), \+ too_big(J, N).
too_big(J, N) :- short(J, N, _).
short(J, N, R) :- demand(J, R, D), capacity(N, R, C), C < D.
most(N, R) :- aggregate_all(max(C, M), capacity(M, R, C), max(_, N)).

== 4. meta-interpreters
# Three, each optional. They run ON the facts and the rules; they do not hold facts or rules of their own.
-- check
# decides and explains: how to say why
explain(short(J, N, R), [N, 'has only', C, 'GB of', R, 'and', J, 'needs', D]) :- demand(J, R, D), capacity(N, R, C), C < D.
-- execute
# acts on the world: what the agent may change by itself, and the command for the device
can_change(runs(J, N)) :- runnable(J, N).
act(runs(J, N), [launch, J, N]).
-- complete
# finds what is missing: what it needs to know before answering, and how to ask it
missing(demand(J, R, _)) :- job(J), member(R, [ram, disk]), \+ demand(J, R, _).
ask(demand(J, R, _), ['How many GB of', R, 'does', J, 'need to run?']).
